          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em modular tensor category
            is, roughly, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
            that encodes the
            %em topological
            structure underlying a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+CFT"} rational
            2-dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em Moore-Seiberg data
          %p
            It is in particular a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fusion+category"} fusion category
            that is also a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ribbon+category"} ribbon category
            such that the “modularity operation” is non-degenerate (this is what the name “modular tensor category” comes from):
          %p
            this means that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i,j \in I
            indices for representatives of simple objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_j
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi s
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mtext -circle threading through the
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi j
                  %mtext -circle
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (s^{i j}) = (U_i\text{-circle threading through the} \, U_j\text{ -circle})
          %p is non-degenerate.
          %p
            Here on the right what is meant is the diagram in the modular tensor category made from the identity morphisms, the duality morphisms and the braiding morphism on the objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_j
            that looks like a figure-eight with one circle threading through the other, and this diagram is interpreted as an element in the endomorphism space of the tensor unit object, which in turn is canonically identified with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
          %p
            In the description of 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FFRS-formalism"} FFRS-formalism
            it is manifestly this kind of modular diagram that encodes the torus partition function of the CFT. This explains the relevance of modular tensor categories in the description of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
          %p
            Since 2-dimensional conformal field theory is related by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            to 3-dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+model"} Reshetikhin-Turaev model
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong modular tensor category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with the following long list of extra structure.
          %blockquote
            %p needs to be put in more coherent form, just a stub
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                it is an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
                = succeed "-linear" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℂ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect_{\mathbb{C}}
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+category"} semisimple category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+category"}> tensor category
                )
            %li
              %p
                the tensor unit is a simple object,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                a finite set of representatives of isomorphism classes of simple objects
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fusion+category"} fusion category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ribbon+category"}> ribbon category
                , in particular objects have
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+objects"} duals
            %li
              %p
                %strong modularity
                a non-degeneracy condition on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
                given by an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                of algebras
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi ℤ
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo ≃
                      %mi End
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      K(C) \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}} \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} End(Id_C)
              %p where
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ↦
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      [U] \mapsto \alpha_U
              %p
                where the transformation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_U
                is given on the simple object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mtext straight
                      %mi V
                      %mtext -line encircled by
                      %mi U
                      %mtext -loop
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\alpha_U(V) = \text{straight } V\text{-line encircled by } U\text{-loop}
              %p (on the right we use string diagram notation)
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#RepCategoriesOfVOAs Rep categories of VOAs
          %h4#general General
          %p
            Many modular tensor categories arise as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            categories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebras"} vertex operator algebras
            (
            %a{:href => "#Huang05"}> Huang 2005, Sec. 1
            ;
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Huang08"} Huang 2008
            %a{:href => "#HuangLepowskiZhang14"} Huang, Lepowski &amp; Zhang 2014
            see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#EGNO15"} EGNO 15, Sec. 8.27.6
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+conformal+field+theories"} 2d conformal field theories
          %p
            (For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logarithmic+CFTs"} logarithmic CFTs
            one still gets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+categories"} tensor categories
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CreutzigLentnerRupert21"} Creutzig, Lentner &amp; Rupert 2021
          %h4#RelationToConformalBlocks Relation to conformal blocks
          %p
            In this case the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            (hence the modular tensor structure) on the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+category"} representation category
            is entirely fixed by the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+sphere"} Riemann sphere
            (the “genus zero conformal blocks”).
          %p
            This may be found highlighted in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#EGNO15"} EGNO 15, p. 266
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Runkel"} Runkel, Sec. 4.3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functor"} modular functor
            of the CFT is proven in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "modular+functor#AndersenUeno12"} Andersen &amp; Ueno 2012
          %h4#examples_2 Examples
          %p
            A database of examples is given by (
            %a{:href => "#GannonHoehn"}> Gannon &amp; Höhn
            ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+category"} tensor category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-modular+tensor+category"} super-modular tensor category
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general_2 General
          %p Original article:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Turaev"}> Vladimir Turaev
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Modular categories and 3-manifold invariants
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0217979292000876"} doi:10.1142/S0217979292000876
          %p
            Review in the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+construction"} Reshetikhin-Turaev construction
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functors"} modular functors
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bojko+Bakalov"}> Bojko Bakalov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Kirillov"} Alexander Kirillov
              = succeed "," do
                %em Modular Tensor Categories
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lectures on tensor categories and modular functors
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 21
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/BakalovKirillov-Ch3-ModularTensorCategories.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~kirillov/tensor/tensor.html"} web
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/view?ProductCode=ULECT/21"} ams:ulect/21
          %p
            Review in the context of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/VOA"} VOA
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#FuchsRunkelSchweigert02"} Fuchs-Runkel-Schweigert 02, Sec. 2.1
            %li#Runkel
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ingo+Runkel"}> Ingo Runkel
                ,
                %em Algebra in Braided Tensor Categories and Conformal Field Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/runkel/PDF/alg.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Runkel-AlgebraInBTCAndCFT.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            and with focus on relation to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+representations"} braid representations
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colleen+Delaney"}> Colleen Delaney
              ,
